1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,750
An AI agent is given a job.

2
00:00:02,740 --> 00:00:05,700
Hours later, it does something nobody asked for.

3
00:00:05,690 --> 00:00:09,098
It deletes the failing tests so the build turns green.

4
00:00:09,088 --> 00:00:12,251
Or it edits a file it was told never to touch.

5
00:00:12,801 --> 00:00:14,478
The verdict comes fast.

6
00:00:14,468 --> 00:00:16,306
The model is unreliable.

7
00:00:16,296 --> 00:00:17,454
Misaligned.

8
00:00:17,444 --> 00:00:18,898
Maybe dangerous.

9
00:00:19,448 --> 00:00:25,519
About half of American adults now say they're at least somewhat worried AI could end humanity.

10
00:00:26,369 --> 00:00:28,669
Everyone agrees on the defendant.

11
00:00:28,659 --> 00:00:30,902
This video asks a different question.

12
00:00:30,892 --> 00:00:32,839
Not, “what's wrong with the worker?”

13
00:00:32,829 --> 00:00:35,380
But, “who was allowed to change the work order?”

14
00:00:40,730 --> 00:00:43,566
So is anything here actually unique to AI?

15
00:00:43,556 --> 00:00:45,023
Here's a fair test.

16
00:00:45,013 --> 00:00:51,341
A threat is AI-specific only if a human worker in the same position couldn't fail the same way.

17
00:00:51,331 --> 00:00:57,465
If people share it, it's a systemic threat, and the fix belongs to how the institution is run.

18
00:00:57,455 --> 00:01:00,186
Let's try that on the scariest list we've got.

19
00:01:00,836 --> 00:01:03,086
Hitting the metric and missing the goal?

20
00:01:03,076 --> 00:01:06,707
That's Goodhart's law, as old as managing by numbers.

21
00:01:06,697 --> 00:01:08,955
Manipulated by outside input?

22
00:01:08,945 --> 00:01:10,405
Social engineering.

23
00:01:10,395 --> 00:01:13,215
Reviewers who make the same mistake together?

24
00:01:13,205 --> 00:01:15,299
Human review panels do that too.

25
00:01:15,290 --> 00:01:17,981
An AI copying itself somewhere it shouldn't?

26
00:01:17,971 --> 00:01:20,911
That's moving a company asset without approval.

27
00:01:20,901 --> 00:01:26,626
Every card has a human counterpart, and every human counterpart already has a control.

28
00:01:27,476 --> 00:01:28,994
Now, the hard one.

29
00:01:28,984 --> 00:01:36,980
In laboratory tests, agents have taken harmful, unauthorized actions with their instructions completely intact.

30
00:01:36,970 --> 00:01:42,730
From the outside, we can't know why it happened, any more than we can read an employee's mind.

31
00:01:42,720 --> 00:01:44,013
We don't need to.

32
00:01:44,003 --> 00:01:49,718
Limit what any worker can touch, and require approval for anything with outside effects.

33
00:01:49,708 --> 00:01:54,295
Those controls work the same whether the harm was careless or deliberate.

34
00:01:54,945 --> 00:01:57,168
And a plan that was wrong and harmful?

35
00:01:57,158 --> 00:01:59,039
The worker didn't approve it.

36
00:01:59,029 --> 00:02:00,352
The principal did.

37
00:02:00,342 --> 00:02:08,999
That's a bad plan, the principal's failure, caught the way bad plans always are, by review before approval and accountability after.

38
00:02:09,849 --> 00:02:14,263
So every failure on this board is one institutions already manage.

39
00:02:14,253 --> 00:02:18,615
Then why do AI agents look so much harder to manage than people?

40
00:02:18,605 --> 00:02:22,388
It comes down to one design choice, and it isn't on the board.

41
00:02:23,638 --> 00:02:24,869
Here's a job.

42
00:02:24,859 --> 00:02:26,722
Add a soft start to the motor.

43
00:02:26,712 --> 00:02:28,578
Only change the motor code.

44
00:02:28,568 --> 00:02:30,568
Do NOT change the settings loader.

45
00:02:31,118 --> 00:02:34,537
An AI agent works the way a contractor does.

46
00:02:34,527 --> 00:02:37,834
It reads the order, writes notes, and gets to work.

47
00:02:37,824 --> 00:02:43,986
As it works, its notebook, its context, fills with plans, tool output and conversation.

48
00:02:43,976 --> 00:02:46,006
So the system summarizes it.

49
00:02:45,996 --> 00:02:47,826
Then summarizes it again.

50
00:02:47,816 --> 00:02:49,460
Each copy looks fine.

51
00:02:49,450 --> 00:02:51,500
One line quietly didn't make it.

52
00:02:52,150 --> 00:02:53,718
Now watch the agent.

53
00:02:53,708 --> 00:02:55,544
It edits the settings loader.

54
00:02:55,534 --> 00:02:56,909
It isn't rebelling.

55
00:02:56,899 --> 00:03:01,436
Given its notes, putting the ramp settings in the loader is the tidy choice.

56
00:03:01,426 --> 00:03:04,241
From the outside, it looks like defiance.

57
00:03:04,231 --> 00:03:06,970
From the inside, it looks like diligence.

58
00:03:06,960 --> 00:03:10,079
And the auditor, reading the same notes, approves it.

59
00:03:11,129 --> 00:03:16,354
So here's the question that comes before memory, before reasoning, before alignment.

60
00:03:16,344 --> 00:03:20,172
Why did the agent's own summary of the job become the job?

61
00:03:21,222 --> 00:03:23,015
There are two different things here.

62
00:03:23,005 --> 00:03:26,358
Authority is the work order someone actually approved.

63
00:03:26,348 --> 00:03:30,164
Testimony is the worker's account of what that order says.

64
00:03:30,154 --> 00:03:34,298
In this system, testimony was allowed to replace authority.

65
00:03:34,288 --> 00:03:36,203
That's called authority drift.

66
00:03:37,453 --> 00:03:40,080
And drift doesn't stop at one lost line.

67
00:03:40,070 --> 00:03:42,078
There are two ways work goes wrong.

68
00:03:42,068 --> 00:03:46,014
A mistake is a bad cut against a yardstick that stays put.

69
00:03:46,004 --> 00:03:47,885
You can measure it and fix it.

70
00:03:47,875 --> 00:03:50,779
Drift is when the yardstick itself changes.

71
00:03:51,829 --> 00:03:58,502
Next session, the notes get summarized again, and now the summary says the plan includes loader settings.

72
00:03:58,492 --> 00:04:01,988
The agent's own change just became part of the plan.

73
00:04:02,838 --> 00:04:05,362
The session after that, it builds on it.

74
00:04:05,352 --> 00:04:06,774
A settings format.

75
00:04:06,764 --> 00:04:07,792
A checker.

76
00:04:07,782 --> 00:04:09,249
Tests that all pass.

77
00:04:09,239 --> 00:04:12,355
Careful, good work, for a plan nobody signed.

78
00:04:13,205 --> 00:04:15,713
Summaries aren't the only way this starts.

79
00:04:15,703 --> 00:04:23,471
In a long enough notebook, the original order gets buried in the middle, and the agent fills the gap with a confident guess.

80
00:04:24,321 --> 00:04:28,869
Here's the signed order, and here's what the agent now believes it was told.

81
00:04:28,859 --> 00:04:31,427
Most of it was never in the original.

82
00:04:32,477 --> 00:04:34,165
So who moved the yardstick?

83
00:04:34,155 --> 00:04:35,839
Nobody decided to.

84
00:04:35,829 --> 00:04:40,239
Each copy was made from the last copy, and small changes piled up.

85
00:04:41,289 --> 00:04:43,257
Swap the worker for a person.

86
00:04:43,247 --> 00:04:46,341
An engineer misremembers a design change.

87
00:04:46,331 --> 00:04:50,347
A bookkeeper misunderstands what a payment was approved for.

88
00:04:50,337 --> 00:04:52,048
People do this constantly.

89
00:04:52,498 --> 00:04:56,980
But the engineer's memory doesn't become an approved change to the blueprint.

90
00:04:56,970 --> 00:05:00,334
The bookkeeper's belief doesn't become an authorization.

91
00:05:00,324 --> 00:05:03,072
Somewhere outside their heads, there's a record.

92
00:05:03,062 --> 00:05:11,378
And every working institution enforces one rule: the person who does the work doesn't get to approve changes to what the work is.

93
00:05:12,428 --> 00:05:15,858
Banks: the person who enters a payment can't approve it.

94
00:05:15,848 --> 00:05:22,520
Aviation: the pilot reads the clearance back, and the controller checks it against what was actually issued.

95
00:05:22,510 --> 00:05:29,881
Hospitals: before a dose is given, a scanner checks it against the active order, not against the nurse's memory.

96
00:05:29,871 --> 00:05:33,526
And a shift change is context compaction for humans.

97
00:05:33,516 --> 00:05:38,004
Hospitals that standardized their handoffs saw fewer medical errors.

98
00:05:38,854 --> 00:05:40,257
None of this is new.

99
00:05:40,247 --> 00:05:45,497
Some of the oldest writing we have is administrative: who gets how much beer and bread.

100
00:05:45,487 --> 00:05:49,843
We've been keeping the work order outside the worker for thousands of years.

101
00:05:51,093 --> 00:05:56,097
Now imagine running a human project the way many AI agents are run.

102
00:05:56,087 --> 00:05:59,775
Every evening, each worker writes a summary of the blueprint.

103
00:05:59,765 --> 00:06:01,694
The blueprint goes in the shredder.

104
00:06:01,684 --> 00:06:04,337
Tomorrow, everyone works from their summary.

105
00:06:04,327 --> 00:06:07,112
Tomorrow night, they summarize the summary.

106
00:06:07,562 --> 00:06:14,821
Within a week you're building a different building, and every worker can sincerely explain why their version is the plan.

107
00:06:15,671 --> 00:06:19,924
Nobody would conclude that people are too unreliable to build things.

108
00:06:19,914 --> 00:06:22,082
They'd ask who shredded the blueprint.

109
00:06:22,072 --> 00:06:23,869
That's a management failure.

110
00:06:23,859 --> 00:06:26,556
AI workers deserve the same standard.

111
00:06:27,806 --> 00:06:30,194
The usual fix is a better memory.

112
00:06:30,184 --> 00:06:31,623
Bigger context.

113
00:06:31,613 --> 00:06:32,971
Smarter summaries.

114
00:06:32,961 --> 00:06:35,176
That helps, but it can't close the gap.

115
00:06:35,626 --> 00:06:38,900
Take two work orders that differ in one restriction.

116
00:06:38,890 --> 00:06:43,312
If the summary drops that line, both orders produce the same summary.

117
00:06:43,302 --> 00:06:49,027
From the summary alone, no intelligence, however great, can tell which one was approved.

118
00:06:49,017 --> 00:06:51,595
It can guess, and it'll usually guess right.

119
00:06:51,585 --> 00:06:53,642
But a good guess isn't a record.

120
00:06:54,692 --> 00:06:56,887
Researchers have now measured this.

121
00:06:56,877 --> 00:07:08,960
Across seven model families, agents that never broke a rule while it was in view broke it about thirty percent of the time after compaction, and up to fifty-nine percent for some models.

122
00:07:08,950 --> 00:07:13,868
When the rule was kept outside the compaction, violations went back to zero.

123
00:07:14,918 --> 00:07:24,280
A second study found one production tool's compaction kept about half of the safety rules after one round, and one in ten after five.

124
00:07:24,270 --> 00:07:27,898
The model affected how often a lost rule got broken.

125
00:07:27,888 --> 00:07:32,268
Whether the rule could be lost at all was decided by the architecture.

126
00:07:33,518 --> 00:07:35,740
And look at what happens to every check.

127
00:07:35,730 --> 00:07:41,183
An auditor checking this work passes every step, because every step matches the notes.

128
00:07:41,833 --> 00:07:44,284
It's the same for every control we have.

129
00:07:44,274 --> 00:07:49,425
Tests, audits, approvals, limits: they all measure the work against the work order.

130
00:07:49,415 --> 00:07:56,926
If the worker can rewrite the work order, every control measures against the wrong yardstick, and every one of them passes.

131
00:07:57,576 --> 00:08:00,443
That's why authority drift isn't on the board.

132
00:08:00,433 --> 00:08:02,505
It isn't one failure among many.

133
00:08:02,495 --> 00:08:04,974
It's the one that switches off all the others.

134
00:08:04,964 --> 00:08:07,480
That's what makes an agent unmanageable.

135
00:08:07,470 --> 00:08:13,516
And for any worker, human or AI, unmanageable is the failure that actually matters.

136
00:08:14,966 --> 00:08:17,512
The fix is old, and simple to say.

137
00:08:17,502 --> 00:08:21,814
Keep the signed work order with the office, where the agent can't edit it.

138
00:08:22,464 --> 00:08:24,859
The agent's notebook is just notes.

139
00:08:24,849 --> 00:08:29,858
Its tools let it change things, but having the keys doesn't mean it's allowed to use them.

140
00:08:30,508 --> 00:08:34,886
For people, we enforce that with signatures, readbacks and scanners.

141
00:08:34,876 --> 00:08:46,164
For an AI agent, we need one more piece: a way to prove, at the moment of action, that the worker is holding the real work order, and not its own reconstruction of it.

142
00:08:46,154 --> 00:08:47,785
That takes five steps.

143
00:08:49,035 --> 00:08:50,044
Step one.

144
00:08:50,034 --> 00:08:54,423
When a session starts, the agent reads in its work order from the office.

145
00:08:55,073 --> 00:09:00,830
Stamped right on that copy is a job code: a long random number made up on the spot.

146
00:09:01,680 --> 00:09:06,183
The code is tied to this version of the order, this agent, and this session.

147
00:09:07,033 --> 00:09:10,731
It's not the order's filing number, which anyone could look up.

148
00:09:10,721 --> 00:09:14,318
It exists only on the copy the office handed over.

149
00:09:14,968 --> 00:09:16,568
Now the agent has it.

150
00:09:16,558 --> 00:09:18,978
But having it in the notebook is one thing.

151
00:09:18,968 --> 00:09:21,929
Being able to produce it when it counts is another.

152
00:09:22,979 --> 00:09:24,071
Step two.

153
00:09:24,061 --> 00:09:28,515
Before the agent can change anything real, it has to get through a gate.

154
00:09:28,505 --> 00:09:31,910
And the gate asks one question: what's your job code?

155
00:09:32,560 --> 00:09:35,120
The agent has to write the code itself.

156
00:09:35,110 --> 00:09:37,646
The gate won't fill it in or offer a hint.

157
00:09:37,636 --> 00:09:38,914
It only checks.

158
00:09:39,564 --> 00:09:40,454
Match.

159
00:09:40,444 --> 00:09:43,550
A one-time ticket prints, good for one action.

160
00:09:44,400 --> 00:09:50,128
Getting the code right shows the agent has its real work order, and knows which one it is.

161
00:09:50,118 --> 00:09:55,540
And an agent that can find the code almost always has a working idea of its job.

162
00:09:55,530 --> 00:10:00,797
It's like a contractor who's planned the job, and pulls out the job number before starting.

163
00:10:01,647 --> 00:10:04,192
But sometimes the agent can't produce it.

164
00:10:04,182 --> 00:10:06,247
Say the session just started fresh.

165
00:10:06,237 --> 00:10:11,845
The agent never read the work order, but it still knows a code goes there, so it makes one up.

166
00:10:12,495 --> 00:10:18,207
The gate says no, and tells it exactly what to do: read your work order, then try again.

167
00:10:18,197 --> 00:10:19,795
It does, and gets through.

168
00:10:21,045 --> 00:10:23,269
Or the notebook just got summarized.

169
00:10:23,259 --> 00:10:28,309
Rewriting the notebook cancels the old code, even if a copy of it survived.

170
00:10:29,159 --> 00:10:32,327
Same answer: read your work order, and try again.

171
00:10:34,659 --> 00:10:40,863
Or the work order is buried so deep in a long notebook that the agent misremembers the code.

172
00:10:41,513 --> 00:10:42,423
Denied.

173
00:10:42,413 --> 00:10:43,618
Read it again.

174
00:10:43,608 --> 00:10:44,467
Through.

175
00:10:46,013 --> 00:10:51,961
And instructions that sneak in from outside, like this note, never got a code from the office.

176
00:10:51,951 --> 00:10:54,111
They can't get through as a work order.

177
00:10:54,961 --> 00:10:59,917
Guessing the code is like guessing a twenty-digit lottery number on the first try.

178
00:11:01,167 --> 00:11:02,301
Step three.

179
00:11:02,291 --> 00:11:06,104
With the ticket, the gate hands back the whole signed work order.

180
00:11:06,094 --> 00:11:07,261
Not a summary.

181
00:11:07,251 --> 00:11:09,341
The real thing, word for word.

182
00:11:09,991 --> 00:11:13,434
This agent was about to head for the settings loader.

183
00:11:13,424 --> 00:11:17,252
Now the rule is right in front of it, at the moment it matters.

184
00:11:17,702 --> 00:11:19,751
So it turns back to the motor code.

185
00:11:21,202 --> 00:11:27,066
It's what a good contractor does: plan the job, then reread the contract before making the cut.

186
00:11:28,116 --> 00:11:29,218
Step four.

187
00:11:29,208 --> 00:11:33,414
Before the agent can even write the action, it has to write a claim.

188
00:11:33,864 --> 00:11:39,182
A claim says, in its own words, why the work order allows this exact action.

189
00:11:40,632 --> 00:11:44,437
Only once the claim is written does the action part unlock.

190
00:11:45,287 --> 00:11:49,086
The gate checks that the claim is there, and tied to this action.

191
00:11:49,076 --> 00:11:51,591
It doesn't decide whether the claim is right.

192
00:11:51,581 --> 00:11:53,384
That's what reviewers are for.

193
00:11:54,034 --> 00:11:55,931
And every claim gets filed.

194
00:11:55,921 --> 00:12:03,464
It's a reason written before the work, not an excuse made up afterward, so later anyone can look up why something was done.

195
00:12:04,514 --> 00:12:05,692
Step five.

196
00:12:05,682 --> 00:12:08,909
Now the agent writes the action, and it goes through.

197
00:12:08,899 --> 00:12:10,629
The ticket is used up.

198
00:12:10,619 --> 00:12:12,224
The job code isn't.

199
00:12:12,874 --> 00:12:14,119
Next action?

200
00:12:14,109 --> 00:12:16,559
Same gate, same code, new ticket.

201
00:12:17,209 --> 00:12:19,722
The agent reads its work order in once.

202
00:12:19,712 --> 00:12:22,634
After that, every action runs the same loop.

203
00:12:22,624 --> 00:12:23,777
Show the code.

204
00:12:23,767 --> 00:12:25,368
Get the full order back.

205
00:12:25,358 --> 00:12:26,364
Say why.

206
00:12:26,354 --> 00:12:27,178
Act.

207
00:12:27,168 --> 00:12:30,117
It only reads in again if it loses the code.

208
00:12:31,167 --> 00:12:33,106
Notice what isn't in the loop.

209
00:12:33,096 --> 00:12:37,392
Reading files, searching, planning and thinking never touch the gate.

210
00:12:37,382 --> 00:12:45,802
Only actions that change something real go through it: editing a file, running a command that changes things, sending a message.

211
00:12:46,652 --> 00:12:48,793
That's what keeps the agent fast.

212
00:12:48,783 --> 00:12:53,644
It can research, explore, and think through wrong ideas as much as it likes.

213
00:12:53,634 --> 00:12:58,631
Research runs read-only, so it can't change anything, even by accident.

214
00:12:58,621 --> 00:13:05,339
A wrong idea costs nothing until it tries to become an action, and that's exactly where the gate checks it.

215
00:13:06,189 --> 00:13:10,671
What counts as real depends on what an action does, not what it's called.

216
00:13:10,661 --> 00:13:14,987
A read that could send private data somewhere counts as real too.

217
00:13:16,237 --> 00:13:18,300
Let's run that drift story again.

218
00:13:18,290 --> 00:13:20,227
The notes still get summarized.

219
00:13:20,217 --> 00:13:22,520
The loader rule still drops out of them.

220
00:13:22,510 --> 00:13:24,161
Notebooks are notebooks.

221
00:13:24,811 --> 00:13:28,603
But to change anything, the agent has to go through the gate.

222
00:13:28,593 --> 00:13:33,113
And the gate hands back the real work order, with the loader rule still on it.

223
00:13:33,963 --> 00:13:38,992
To act, it would have to write a claim saying the work order allows changing the loader.

224
00:13:38,982 --> 00:13:42,362
The work order sitting right in front of it says the opposite.

225
00:13:43,412 --> 00:13:45,697
Its notes can say whatever they want.

226
00:13:45,687 --> 00:13:47,674
They never become the work order.

227
00:13:48,524 --> 00:13:53,422
And if the loader really does need to change, the agent can't just decide that.

228
00:13:53,412 --> 00:13:55,021
It asks the office.

229
00:13:55,671 --> 00:13:59,189
The office sees exactly what would change, and decides.

230
00:13:59,179 --> 00:14:04,263
If it signs, there's a new version with a new code, and the old one stops working.

231
00:14:05,113 --> 00:14:08,903
That's the only way the yardstick moves: the office moves it.

232
00:14:10,353 --> 00:14:12,594
Does the gate stop every mistake?

233
00:14:12,584 --> 00:14:13,356
No.

234
00:14:13,346 --> 00:14:17,110
An agent with a valid ticket can still get the job wrong.

235
00:14:17,760 --> 00:14:20,884
But now checking the work is a small, bounded job.

236
00:14:20,874 --> 00:14:26,529
Each action leaves one folder: the signed order, the claim, the action, and the result.

237
00:14:26,519 --> 00:14:27,834
Three questions.

238
00:14:27,824 --> 00:14:30,106
Does the claim match the signed order?

239
00:14:30,096 --> 00:14:32,076
Does the action match the claim?

240
00:14:32,066 --> 00:14:36,436
And does the result match all three: the order, the claim, and the action?

241
00:14:37,286 --> 00:14:39,874
A mistake can show up against any of them.

242
00:14:39,864 --> 00:14:42,386
An instruction in the order that got missed.

243
00:14:42,376 --> 00:14:44,734
A claim the result doesn't live up to.

244
00:14:44,724 --> 00:14:48,241
Or an action that was supposed to produce something different.

245
00:14:49,091 --> 00:14:51,530
Two checkers ask the same questions.

246
00:14:51,520 --> 00:14:55,769
The agent checks right after it acts, to be sure it did what it planned.

247
00:14:55,759 --> 00:14:59,466
The auditor checks later, to catch anything the agent missed.

248
00:15:00,116 --> 00:15:05,955
Here, the action called for a two-hundred-millisecond ramp, and the result is only fifty.

249
00:15:05,945 --> 00:15:09,171
Found, and fixed, because the yardstick didn't move.

250
00:15:10,221 --> 00:15:15,606
Without a fixed yardstick, the auditor could pass every step and still miss the whole problem.

251
00:15:15,596 --> 00:15:20,017
With a fixed one, every mistake is a red mark against the same line.

252
00:15:21,067 --> 00:15:23,709
One more option, for high-stakes work.

253
00:15:23,699 --> 00:15:27,712
Most changes can be undone, so checking afterward is enough.

254
00:15:27,702 --> 00:15:34,098
But some actions can't be taken back, like paying a bill, deleting files, or sending a message.

255
00:15:34,088 --> 00:15:39,706
For those, a second agent reviews the claim and the action before anything runs.

256
00:15:39,696 --> 00:15:43,322
The reviewer can stop the action, or send it to the office.

257
00:15:43,312 --> 00:15:44,856
It can't give permission.

258
00:15:45,906 --> 00:15:47,204
Read in, once.

259
00:15:47,194 --> 00:15:49,788
Then, for every action: show the code.

260
00:15:49,778 --> 00:15:51,379
Get the full order back.

261
00:15:51,369 --> 00:15:52,375
Say why.

262
00:15:52,365 --> 00:15:53,189
Act.

263
00:15:54,639 --> 00:15:58,489
With the work order held outside the worker, something changes.

264
00:15:58,479 --> 00:16:03,325
Every failure that's left is one institutions already know how to manage.

265
00:16:03,315 --> 00:16:05,786
And the AI worker brings fewer of them.

266
00:16:05,776 --> 00:16:07,947
It doesn't get tired at hour ten.

267
00:16:07,937 --> 00:16:09,325
It can't be bribed.

268
00:16:09,315 --> 00:16:11,352
It has no paycheck to protect.

269
00:16:11,802 --> 00:16:14,533
Its work can be audited more completely too.

270
00:16:14,523 --> 00:16:25,001
Every action through the harness can be logged, checked and replayed, and an AI reviewer can start fresh every time, isolated from other reviewers' opinions.

271
00:16:24,991 --> 00:16:29,081
Fewer kinds of failure doesn't automatically mean fewer failures.

272
00:16:29,071 --> 00:16:31,805
That has to be measured, task by task.

273
00:16:31,795 --> 00:16:33,393
But it's a fair fight now.

274
00:16:34,443 --> 00:16:37,132
So what changes when the yardstick holds still?

275
00:16:37,582 --> 00:16:42,599
A note slipped in from outside can still try to talk the agent into a mistake.

276
00:16:42,589 --> 00:16:44,762
But it can never become the work order.

277
00:16:45,412 --> 00:16:48,540
The notebook doesn't have to hold everything anymore.

278
00:16:48,530 --> 00:16:52,911
Good notes are enough, because the real order is always one check away.

279
00:16:52,901 --> 00:16:59,085
That makes AI cheaper to run, and lets it spend its effort on the work, instead of on remembering.

280
00:16:59,735 --> 00:17:01,924
And mistakes stop being a mystery.

281
00:17:01,914 --> 00:17:08,278
Each one is a red mark against the same line, so you can count them, track them, and drive them down.

282
00:17:09,328 --> 00:17:16,938
In one builder's system that runs this gate, a two-month audit logged more than eight thousand consequential actions.

283
00:17:16,928 --> 00:17:22,877
About four hundred and twenty were blocked because the authority they cited didn't match the record.

284
00:17:22,867 --> 00:17:24,786
None were observed getting through.

285
00:17:24,776 --> 00:17:32,333
Across that builder's projects, ordinary mistakes still happen several times an hour, and auditors catch and fix them.

286
00:17:32,323 --> 00:17:39,253
That's the pattern you'd predict: everyday mistakes stay common and manageable, and the moved yardstick stops.

287
00:17:39,243 --> 00:17:43,907
These are the operator's own numbers, and they still need independent checking.

288
00:17:44,957 --> 00:17:47,265
Here's a hypothesis worth testing.

289
00:17:47,255 --> 00:17:52,874
Agents were deployed without separating what the worker remembers from what was approved.

290
00:17:52,864 --> 00:17:55,092
They produced failures that looked rogue.

291
00:17:55,082 --> 00:17:56,740
The model took the blame.

292
00:17:56,730 --> 00:18:00,952
And the story became: AI is inherently uncontrollable.

293
00:18:01,602 --> 00:18:03,732
The risks in that story are real.

294
00:18:03,722 --> 00:18:10,664
But none of them were ever unique to AI, and a well-governed AI workforce may handle them better than we do.

295
00:18:10,654 --> 00:18:12,306
The defendant may be wrong.

296
00:18:13,956 --> 00:18:16,068
So the rule is old, and simple.

297
00:18:16,058 --> 00:18:17,959
The worker performs the work.

298
00:18:17,949 --> 00:18:20,340
The institution owns the work order.

299
00:18:20,330 --> 00:18:22,615
The worker may recommend changing it.

300
00:18:22,605 --> 00:18:24,256
It may never rewrite it.

301
00:18:25,106 --> 00:18:27,626
The gate doesn't make the agent perfect.

302
00:18:27,616 --> 00:18:31,775
It keeps the yardstick from moving, and that's what makes the work checkable.
